frigg's slab_pool hands out the objects of a new slab as they are asked
for. It linked every object of a slab into its free list when it made
the slab, which wrote to every page of it: a program that allocated one
small block of each of nine sizes held nine quarter-megabyte slabs, 576
pages, in memory from its start, faulted in and zeroed one at a time,
then shared with every child it forked and freed by every child that
exited. A slab now keeps the address of its first object never handed
out; a freed object is taken first, then the next fresh one, so a slab
takes memory as glibc's heap does, as it is used. Meant for upstream,
in frigg.

